# feature-flow: recommended CLAUDE.md rules
#
# Copy the sections you want into ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md (applies to every project)
# or into a project's CLAUDE.md (applies to that repo only), then replace every
# <placeholder>. Delete what does not fit your team.
#
# Without these rules feature-flow stays conservative: it asks before committing
# or pushing, and it hands UI checks and review follow-ups back to you.

## 1. Decision gate before plan approval
Before asking me to approve a spec or plan, output these three sections and wait
for my answer on section 2 before writing code:
1. **Existing mechanisms to reuse**: how this repo already solves the nearest
   equivalent problem (with file:line). Build something new only if nothing fits,
   and say why.
2. **Decisions that need sign-off** (at most 5): options, your recommendation, and
   the evidence (repo convention, spec, prior MR/PR). Always list: cross-service
   transport, which layer owns the data, migrations, where docs live.
3. **Out of scope**.

## 2. Definition of done
- Report "done" only with evidence: real build/lint/test output; new tests fail
  when the fix is removed.
- UI changes: drive the UI yourself with Playwright on <test environment, e.g.
  staging URL or test device> and capture screenshots. Do not hand me a list of
  manual test steps unless the step needs physical access or an environment you
  cannot reach; say which.
- MR/PR done = CI green + mergeable + every review thread answered by a commit
  or reply.
- Never skip hooks (--no-verify, SKIP=, core.hooksPath=/dev/null). If forced to,
  say which hook and why in the first line of the report.
- Design against the latest origin/<base branch>, not a possibly stale local
  checkout.

## 3. Watch reviews yourself
After sending an MR/PR for review, watch it with Monitor and a polling command:
    <your watcher, e.g. a script that polls the MR/PR API every 2 minutes and
     prints one line per new comment / pipeline change / mergeable state>
- New comment from <review bot or reviewer>: fix, reply on the thread (do not
  resolve it), hand it back for review, keep watching.
- Merge conflict / needs rebase: rebase yourself.
- Ready (CI green, mergeable, no open threads): only then notify me.
- Stop and ask only when a comment changes the architecture.

## 4. Pre-authorized actions (do these without asking)
On feature branches and test environments:
- commit, push, rebase, open a draft MR/PR, update its description
- reply to review threads, hand the MR/PR back to the reviewer
- deploy to <test environment> (back up first)
- run build/test/lint, retry flaky CI jobs
- <personal repos where pushing straight to main is fine>

Always ask first:
- the decision gate (section 1)
- changes to main/base branches, merging, deleting others' branches/worktrees
- changes to public API / protocol definitions, dropping databases
- anything that affects environments other than <test environment>

## 5. Approval granularity
- Present brainstorming/spec/design as one complete version for one approval,
  not section by section.
- Do not end replies with "shall I ...?" for actions pre-authorized in section 4.
- Before long or overnight runs, list the commands that might stop on a
  permission prompt so I can allowlist them once.
